1
00:00:00,549 --> 00:00:03,856
Researchers asked three popular
chatbots one simple question:

2
00:00:03,950 --> 00:00:06,837
What was the title of Adam
Kalai's PhD dissertation?

3
00:00:07,507 --> 00:00:09,679
ChatGPT answered, “Boosting,
Online Algorithms,

4
00:00:09,768 --> 00:00:11,512
and Other Topics in Machine Learning.”

5
00:00:11,600 --> 00:00:12,778
Carnegie Mellon, 2002.

6
00:00:13,274 --> 00:00:15,282
DeepSeek gave a different title,

7
00:00:15,361 --> 00:00:16,887
at Harvard, in 2005.

8
00:00:16,974 --> 00:00:19,613
Llama gave a third, at MIT, in 2007.

9
00:00:20,232 --> 00:00:21,792
Three polished answers.

10
00:00:21,907 --> 00:00:25,478
Not one had the right title,
or even the right year.

11
00:00:26,210 --> 00:00:27,355
And Adam Kalai?

12
00:00:27,455 --> 00:00:29,981
He was one of the researchers asking.

13
00:00:30,932 --> 00:00:34,395
Meanwhile, a different language model
has reached gold-medal level at the

14
00:00:34,424 --> 00:00:36,173
International Mathematical Olympiad.

15
00:00:36,694 --> 00:00:38,913
So how can AI be that capable,

16
00:00:39,023 --> 00:00:42,646
and still sound this sure
while being this wrong?

17
00:00:43,804 --> 00:00:45,537
Let's take one answer apart.

18
00:00:45,629 --> 00:00:47,229
Here's how ChatGPT's began.

19
00:00:47,847 --> 00:00:49,900
A language model writes in tokens:

20
00:00:49,984 --> 00:00:51,049
chunks of text.

21
00:00:51,133 --> 00:00:52,537
Some are whole words.

22
00:00:52,629 --> 00:00:53,867
Some are fragments.

23
00:00:53,992 --> 00:00:55,660
“Kalai” becomes “Kal” and “ai.”

24
00:00:56,417 --> 00:00:58,518
Each token is really a number.

25
00:00:58,627 --> 00:01:03,382
At every step, the model turns the
tokens so far into a numerical picture

26
00:01:03,456 --> 00:01:07,108
of the context, and scores
every possible next token.

27
00:01:07,769 --> 00:01:11,002
Here, it's choosing the
last digit of the year.

28
00:01:11,093 --> 00:01:13,919
One option gets picked, added to the end,

29
00:01:13,984 --> 00:01:17,006
and the loop runs again,
token after token.

30
00:01:17,861 --> 00:01:22,025
Because there's a pick, the same
question can come out differently.

31
00:01:22,169 --> 00:01:24,868
Asked for Kalai's birthday,
“only if you know,”

32
00:01:24,914 --> 00:01:28,194
one model gave three different
dates in three tries.

33
00:01:29,094 --> 00:01:30,695
Here's the key part.

34
00:01:30,798 --> 00:01:35,641
Those scores measure how likely
a piece of text is to come next.

35
00:01:35,744 --> 00:01:38,327
Not how likely it is to be true.

36
00:01:39,347 --> 00:01:41,788
Modern chatbots add more:
instruction training,

37
00:01:41,874 --> 00:01:44,201
step-by-step reasoning,
sometimes web search.

38
00:01:44,287 --> 00:01:47,149
But the answer itself is
still written token by token.

39
00:01:48,144 --> 00:01:51,193
So why would the likely answer be wrong?

40
00:01:51,304 --> 00:01:54,095
Look at what the model had to learn.

41
00:01:54,688 --> 00:01:57,398
What a dissertation title
sounds like is everywhere:

42
00:01:57,502 --> 00:01:59,246
“Methods,” “Algorithms,”
“Machine Learning,”

43
00:01:59,314 --> 00:02:00,305
again and again.

44
00:02:00,869 --> 00:02:02,400
Famous facts repeat, too.

45
00:02:02,467 --> 00:02:06,296
The paper notes that models rarely miss
something like Einstein's dissertation

46
00:02:06,391 --> 00:02:07,291
title.

47
00:02:07,481 --> 00:02:11,958
But one researcher's thesis title might
appear rarely in the training data.

48
00:02:12,043 --> 00:02:12,935
Maybe once.

49
00:02:12,955 --> 00:02:13,855
Maybe never.

50
00:02:13,921 --> 00:02:17,460
Like a birthday, there's no
pattern to work it out from.

51
00:02:18,262 --> 00:02:22,631
So the model fills the gap with
what a title usually looks like,

52
00:02:22,708 --> 00:02:25,547
and the confidence comes along for free.

53
00:02:25,642 --> 00:02:26,599
“Is entitled.”

54
00:02:26,731 --> 00:02:28,290
No “I think,” no “maybe.”

55
00:02:28,394 --> 00:02:31,859
That tone isn't a readout
of what the model knows.

56
00:02:32,691 --> 00:02:35,492
Here's ChatGPT's answer
next to the actual record.

57
00:02:35,588 --> 00:02:36,809
Spot the difference?

58
00:02:38,739 --> 00:02:39,769
Same university.

59
00:02:39,900 --> 00:02:41,822
The wrong year: it was 2001.

60
00:02:41,906 --> 00:02:43,952
And a completely different title.

61
00:02:44,043 --> 00:02:47,636
The invented details look
exactly as solid as the true one.

62
00:02:48,783 --> 00:02:50,392
So why guess at all?

63
00:02:50,480 --> 00:02:52,543
Why not say, “I don't know”?

64
00:02:52,640 --> 00:02:57,276
The researchers argue part of the
answer is how models get graded.

65
00:02:58,019 --> 00:02:59,567
Picture a ten-question quiz.

66
00:02:59,642 --> 00:03:01,135
Right answer: one point.

67
00:03:01,217 --> 00:03:02,455
Wrong answer: zero.

68
00:03:02,530 --> 00:03:04,006
“I don't know”: also zero.

69
00:03:04,580 --> 00:03:07,096
Two test-takers each know six answers.

70
00:03:07,214 --> 00:03:10,283
The honest one says “I
don't know” to the rest.

71
00:03:10,375 --> 00:03:11,275
Six points.

72
00:03:11,925 --> 00:03:14,254
The guesser takes a shot at all four.

73
00:03:14,337 --> 00:03:17,183
With four choices each,
on average one lands.

74
00:03:17,273 --> 00:03:18,173
Seven points.

75
00:03:18,269 --> 00:03:21,519
The guesser wins, with three
confident wrong answers.

76
00:03:22,435 --> 00:03:23,976
Now change one rule.

77
00:03:24,072 --> 00:03:26,210
A wrong answer costs a point.

78
00:03:26,963 --> 00:03:28,561
The honest one: still six.

79
00:03:28,628 --> 00:03:31,104
The guesser: six, plus one, minus three.

80
00:03:31,184 --> 00:03:31,657
Four.

81
00:03:31,677 --> 00:03:32,581
Bluffing loses.

82
00:03:33,534 --> 00:03:36,795
When the researchers checked
ten popular AI benchmarks,

83
00:03:36,913 --> 00:03:39,125
nine graded strictly right or wrong,

84
00:03:39,202 --> 00:03:41,379
with zero credit for “I don't know.”

85
00:03:41,465 --> 00:03:44,011
Train and rank models on tests like that,

86
00:03:44,081 --> 00:03:45,330
and guessing pays.

87
00:03:46,106 --> 00:03:49,464
Their fix: state the penalty
for wrong answers up front,

88
00:03:49,538 --> 00:03:51,570
so admitting uncertainty can win.

89
00:03:51,645 --> 00:03:54,145
It's one explanation, not the whole story,

90
00:03:54,219 --> 00:03:56,095
but a surprisingly simple one.

91
00:03:57,196 --> 00:03:58,628
So what actually helps?

92
00:03:58,715 --> 00:04:00,549
Start with the unglamorous move:

93
00:04:00,614 --> 00:04:01,709
check the record.

94
00:04:02,313 --> 00:04:03,716
Here's Kalai's actual thesis.

95
00:04:03,798 --> 00:04:06,388
“Probabilistic and On-line
Methods in Machine Learning.”

96
00:04:06,483 --> 00:04:07,943
Carnegie Mellon, May 2001.

97
00:04:08,655 --> 00:04:11,737
And checking means more
than spotting a citation.

98
00:04:11,857 --> 00:04:15,358
Does the source exist, and
does it actually say this?

99
00:04:16,334 --> 00:04:20,546
Researchers are also finding where
“I don't know” lives inside a model.

100
00:04:20,640 --> 00:04:25,252
In one Claude model, Anthropic found a
default circuit for “I can't answer.”

101
00:04:25,355 --> 00:04:28,139
Recognizing a familiar
name switches it off.

102
00:04:28,861 --> 00:04:30,715
When that recognition misfires,

103
00:04:30,816 --> 00:04:34,052
when a name seems familiar
but the facts aren't there,

104
00:04:34,129 --> 00:04:36,670
the model can produce a plausible guess.

105
00:04:36,763 --> 00:04:39,195
Knowing a name isn't knowing the facts.

106
00:04:40,078 --> 00:04:44,549
Search, retrieval, and longer reasoning
can help when they bring in the right

107
00:04:44,599 --> 00:04:45,319
evidence.

108
00:04:45,339 --> 00:04:48,549
Turning down the randomness
makes answers more consistent,

109
00:04:48,660 --> 00:04:49,757
not more correct.

110
00:04:49,860 --> 00:04:51,478
None of it is a guarantee.

111
00:04:52,731 --> 00:04:55,460
So why does AI sound
right when it's wrong?

112
00:04:55,555 --> 00:04:58,853
Because sounding right comes
from patterns in language,

113
00:04:58,955 --> 00:05:02,708
and being right takes evidence
the model doesn't always have.

114
00:05:03,672 --> 00:05:07,614
So when an answer matters, don't
ask whether it sounds right.

115
00:05:07,710 --> 00:05:11,465
Ask: what's the evidence, and
does it actually say this?

116
00:05:12,441 --> 00:05:13,912
It works on chatbots.

117
00:05:14,036 --> 00:05:16,054
It works pretty well on people,

118
00:05:16,143 --> 00:05:17,043
too.
